Dynamic Mechanical Analysis (DMA) is valuable in the development of composite materials because it provides precise measurements of the variation in stiffness and damping properties with temperature and vibration frequency. The results accurately pinpoint the Glass Transition Temperature (Tg​) of the polymer matrix, which represents the maximum safe operating temperature for the material before it softens and loses its structural integrity. Furthermore, DMA distinguishes between the elastic and viscous components of the response, offering critical insight into the composite’s ability to withstand operational stresses, reduce vibration, and maintain long-term reliability in demanding applications. Ng et al. ( 34 ) used ball milling to obtain NBC with an average diameter of 80–90 nm and used it as a filler in poly(3-hydroxybutyrate-co-3hydroxhexanoate) (PHBHHx) polymer. The onset of thermal degradation, crystallisation temperature, and degree of crystallinity all increased with the addition of 4 wt% NBC. Mechanical property enhancements were confirmed by tensile testing, with tensile modulus and strength increasing by 12.5% and 13%, respectively. DMA analysis showed that, for NBC loading up to 3 wt%, the stiffness (storage modulus) of the PHBHHx composite increased. Crucially, this enhanced stiffness was maintained up to 100 °C, suggesting suitability for high-temperature applications. Furthermore, the composites’ damping factor ( tan δ \documentclass[12pt]{minimal}
