The viscoelastic properties and glass transition temperature (Tg) of the nanocomposites were evaluated using DMA. Figure  13  displays the tensile storage modulus (E’) of the tested samples and Table  2  summarise the values. E’ represents the elastic component of a viscoelastic material and is a measure of its stiffness. For all composites, E’ decreased gradually with increasing temperature until the glass transition point was reached; thereafter, it was decreased rapidly before stabilizing in the rubber region. The composite exhibited three distinct phases: the glassy region, the transition region, and the rubbery region. In the glassy region, the polymer chains were tightly packed with limited mobility, resulting in a high E’. During the transition region, the polymer chains absorbed sufficient thermal energy to move more freely. As the temperature approached Tg, chain disentanglement and increased free volume led to a pronounced reduction in E’. In the rubbery region, E’ for all nanocomposites remained stable. Incorporation of nanoparticles increased E’, confirming that addition of nanofillers to a polymer matrix enhance the stiffness. For CNC nanocomposites, the maximum E’ occurred at 5 wt%, after which decreased at 7 wt% due to agglomeration. A similar trend was observed for NBC nanocomposites, where E’ peaked at 10 wt% and decreased at 15 wt% due to agglomeration. Fig. 13 Tensile storage modulus and tan δ curves for the CNC and NBC nanocomposites Table 2 Glass transition temperature, tensile storage modulus, and tan δ of the CNC and NBC nanocomposites. Superscript letters indicate significant differences (p < 0.05) based on Welch’s t-test analysis; groups sharing the same letter are statistically indistinguishable CNC NBC Loading (wt%) E’ (MPa) tan δ Tg (°C) V c ( 10 - 3 m o l / c m 3 ) \documentclass[12pt]{minimal}
